I imagine that at this point in time not  too many people need an introduction to  the trolley problem that gets used in  ethics and also in moral psychology and  other related fields but we may as well  do a little bit of review very quickly  so the classic trolley problem is a  moral dilemma you see that person there  and he's got a lever which changes the  track that the trolley would run on the  trolley is by default running down the  track where it will soon run over and  kill five people but if the person pulls  the lever he can save the day in a  certain sense for at least those five  people by having the trolley go and run  over the other person who's tied to the  track the question then is what should a  person do when placed in such  circumstances  the trolley problem has given rise to a  lot of discussions in moral philosophy  and in related fields and it's also been  an incredibly fertile spur or which we  call it catalyst for memes and this is  actually one of my favorite ones right  here those of you who are up on meme  culture will no doubt recognize the this  bad boy can fit so much X in it meme  going on here and I think that this is  really fitting for the trolley problem  because there's there's people who've  made whole careers out of it I actually  don't think of that great of an example  or a dilemma myself but I can recognize  that you can sure get a lot of mileage  out of it  here's the second meme and this actually  ties into a variant of the trolley  problem in which you don't switch the  tracks but instead push a fat person off  of a bridge onto the tracks thereby  stopping the trolley but producing that  person's death in order to save the  other five people and I kind of like  this one because it doesn't say well you  know do you push the fat guy off instead  it says well you're a fat guy or a fat  person do you push the fat person off or  do you jump yourself and that's actually  one of the possibilities in that variant  of the problem 