om a  utilitarian perspective we'd probably  actually prefer that the five undergo  that them that the one undergo the  gruesome death but if we look at it from  other perspectives we might say well you  know we're transgressing some right so  this is an interesting little wrinkle on  it isn't it  Meem number five that i selected is even  funnier I think there's at least if you  enjoy dark humor so there's a mad  scientist who's obsessed with trolley  problems and he's devised one and given  you the lever the trolley will run over  five people but if you divert it it was  instead travel back in time and kill  Philippa foot before she can propose the  original trolley problem do you pull and  of course I mean you know if we consider  this from utilitarian perspective it's  really no different than any other one  but the fact that we are you know  playing around with time-travel and  saying maybe we'll destroy trolley  problems altogether by eliminating the  person who originally formulated it  there's something else going on there  that's kind of funny  number six shows us the adaptation of  one dilemma to another dilemma both of  which are you could say 20th century  classic dilemmas this has to do of  course with Sophie's Choice where a  woman is given the choice of whether her  one child or another child will die and  is told if she doesn't choose then both  will die and there's a way of  representing this in terms of trolley  problems so you can divert the trolley  towards your son or towards your  daughter if you choose neither both of  them will die what do you pick and this  is indeed a terrible dilemma to be  placed in from utilitarian perspective  you'd you'd have to pick one of them but  from a deontological perspective you  might end up having both of them die  because you refuse to choose  now this is a really interesting and  telling one I think so you've got five  people lined up on the tracks either way  and you've got the lever and the default  is that the trolley rolls over five  people that you love and you can change  it to run over five people that love you  so starting off who do you value more  the people that you love yourself or  people that love you the same issue of  whether you pull the lever or not comes  up here as well and it's not clear  exactly how you should decide between  five of one and five of another I think  to some people it might seem kind of a  no-brainer well obviously you should  save the people that you love or else  you don't really love them why would you  pick people who love you instead but you  know it's kind of an arbitrary thing  maybe you don't love them for the right  reasons or you know it's very subjective  isn't it that's just the mere fact that  you happen to love these people as a  reason to give them priority  here's a particularly funny variant of  it obviously there's no trolley yet  there you've just got you people are  hanging out on the train tracks you  usually hang out on do you ask to join  the large group which seems more fun  join the singular person for a more  intimate connection or does your social  anxiety force you to just go home  obviously if your social anxiety does  that you're better off you can evade the  problem altogether but otherwise it  looks like you're going to be stuck  there on the tracks with somebody and  the good that you thought you're  generating well it's in the hands of  somebody else who maybe you'll flip the  lever or won't  for number nine I thought that I would  select one that actually has nine  different parts to it and this is I  think most of you will recognize and  alignment chart that's originally coming  from D&amp;D but has been turned into so  many other things in meme culture so  let's run our way through all these  different alignments we got lawful good  I want to save you but I cannot commit  murder so they don't pull the lever  neutral good I will save you even if I  have to go to prison for this taking it  on them